me, modesk =±1contain90 .68%of the error energy. Splitting\\n803: each modal energy as\\n804: |rk−uk|2 = (|rk|−|u k|)2 + 2|rk||uk|(1−cos(argr k−argu k))\\n805: gives a phase contribution of99.83%in that example. The second summand is defined as zero\\n806: when either magnitude is zero. Across the 48 global runs, the phase fraction at peak ranges from\\n807: 86.48%to100 .00%, whereas the fraction in modes±1ranges from0 .81%to96 .77%. Low-mode\\n808: concentration is therefore case-dependent. The residual proof allows coherent accumulation and\\n809: does not assume statistically independent step errors.\\n810: The main campaign took 84.3 seconds and peaked at 390 MB resident memory on the recorded\\n811: single-thread local runtime. The accompanying submission package contains this article and its\\n812: editable source, the numerical library, tests, scripts, seeds, configurations, compact scalar summaries\\n813: and vector figures. Its README gives installation, test and experiment commands that regenerate\\n814: all complex Fourier checkpoints, step defects and reference comparisons. These generated arrays\\n815: remain in the local results directory and are excluded from version control. The theorem concerns\\n816: the exact time-semidiscrete update; a fully discrete uniform convergence theorem and a rate lower\\n817: bound are outside its scope.\\n818: 14\\n819: \\n820: References\\n821: [1] B. Li and Y. Wu,An Unfiltered Low-Regularity Integrator for the KdV Equation with So-\\n822: lutions Below H1, Foundations of Computational Mathematics26(2026), 1321–1380. DOI:\\n823: 10.1007/s10208-025-09702-0. The equation, lemma, and page references in this article use the\\n824: 50-page author manuscript athttps://www.polyu.edu.hk/ama/profile/byli/FoCM-3.pdf.\\n825: [2] A.V.Babin, A.A.IlyinandE.S.Titi,On the regularization mechanism for the periodic Korteweg–\\n826: de Vries equation, arXiv:0910.1389v2(23October2010), https://arxiv.org/abs/0910.1389v2.\\n827: All theorem locators refer to this 42-page version.\\n828: [3]MColbrook/AIM ,518. Removing the logarithmic loss in the Li–Wu KdV integrator, fixed problem\\n829: statement at commitc9929805cece044705abaaebe1cebddac1ba11a6ofMColbrook/AIM.\\n830: [4] The SciPy community, scipy.integrate.solve_ivp documentation, https://docs.scipy\\n831: .org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.integrate.solve_ivp.html , accessed 10\\n832: October 2026.\\n833: AI use declaration\\n834: ChatGPT was used to develop candidate arguments in the source research conversation. Codex\\n835: assisted with reconstructing that material, expanding and auditing the natural-language proof,\\n836: checking primary sources and citation applicability, rewriting the manuscript, and implementing\\n837: and testing the numerical experiments. A separate fresh-context AI review was used to look for\\n838: mathematical and evidential objections. These natural-language and computational checks do not\\n839: constitute formal verification or human peer review.\\n840: 15\"}"}
{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/nl0-format.md\", \"total_lines\": 426, \"start\": 251, \"end\": 426, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 53, \"requested_end\": 426, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"251: ## Inference fields\\n252: \\n253: Required fields: `id: Rddd`, `inputs` (unique proposition IDs), `output` (one\\n254: N/P ID), `definitions` (direct D references), `rules` (permitted logic-rule\\n255: names), `locals`, `argument`, and `applications`.\\n256: \\n257: - `locals`: `{symbol, type, scope}` records. Scope identifies a binding inside\\n258:   this inference; names cannot shadow globals. Two bindings in different\\n259:   scopes/nodes may use the same spelling. Bound variables of source theorems\\n260:   can be alpha-renamed and instantiated. Audit actual binding/freshness.\\n261:   In v2 also require `start`, `end` (inclusive S IDs), `kind` (`arbitrary`,\\n262:   `definition`, or `witness`), `declaration` (full introduction), `from_inputs`,\\n263:   `from_local` (prior F IDs) and `objects` (already available objects used to\\n264:   introduce this one). A witness must have an input/local existence basis.\\n265:   The checker verifies declared availability/order, not the existence claim's\\n266:   semantics. Source-bound variables need a legitimate local instantiation.\\n267:   The `scope` label and inclusive start/end must agree with the prose. Keep an\\n268:   object live through its last actual use. Establish an existence F in an\\n269:   earl…1404 tokens truncated…de, carrier,\\n346: prior_exposure, read_materials}`. Modes are `unperformed`,\\n347: `same_context_diagnostic`, and `fresh_packet_only`. A passing replay/overall\\n348: inference requires the actual fresh carrier, empty prior proof exposure, and\\n349: `read_materials` equal to `[\\\"packet:<hash>\\\"]` for mathematical input. Skill\\n350: instructions supply the procedure, not additional mathematics. The hash must\\n351: match the regenerated packet. Keep traces as evidence; metadata cannot\\n352: authenticate a claimed identity or prove truthful isolation.\\n353: \\n354: Format 4 may additionally bind `review_trace:{path,sha256}` for actual tool logs.\\n355: This is execution metadata, not a native theorem source or an extra premise.\\n356: `library_evidence` contains only the successful pinned evidence of explicitly\\n357: retained native mathlib notes; ordinary mathematical notes require none.\\n358: \\n359: `audit-template` creates only UNVERIFIED reviews, empty check locations and an\\n360: unperformed replay. It refuses to overwrite an existing output. It does not\\n361: inherit author notes or old verdicts. `inspect-audit` can inspect this pending\\n362: record; `check --audit` rejects it. Each PASS must come from actual review.\\n363: \\n364: `check --audit` accepts only fully passing records. `inspect-audit --audit`\\n365: also validates the completeness and evidence of FAIL/UNVERIFIED records and\\n366: returns their unresolved review paths; success of inspection is not proof\\n367: acceptance. Overall PASS with a failed child review never passes acceptance.\\n368: \\n369: Mode `same_context_self_audit` requires `independent: false`. If claiming an\\n370: independent review, disclose its actual carrier and input boundary; this file\\n371: does not authenticate identity or prove independence.\\n372: \\n373: ## Local packet\\n374: \\n375: The v2 packet contains `contract_version`, `case_id`, spec/graph hashes,\\n376: parameters, fixed logic, pins, expanded definitions, complete input/output\\n377: ports, the current inference, a warning, and `packet_sha256`. Source ports keep\\n378: applicability obligations and M signatures; primary-document verification is\\n379: separate. Producer arguments, original-proof locations, source proof text,\\n380: non-input H and extra ancestor/guard statements are not supplied. Guards must\\n381: already be direct inputs. The packet intentionally omits the global full target\\n382: so that its unstated premises cannot become an ambient local hypothesis.\\n383: \\n384: Its digest is SHA256 of UTF-8 canonical JSON (`sort_keys=True`,\\n385: `ensure_ascii=False`, `separators=(\\\",\\\", \\\":\\\")`) excluding `packet_sha256`.\\n386: It is independent of audit-verdict fields, avoiding a circular receipt hash.\\n387: Packets are generated views and never a second editable proof authority.\\n388: \\n389: ## Mechanical boundaries\\n390: \\n391: The checker validates declared objects, the frozen contract, graph closure,\\n392: notation-name metadata, citation parity, local-assumption accounting, guarded\\n393: definition availability, and hash-bound audit coverage. It verifies source\\n394: checkout/file hashes, not the truth of a source restatement. The printed\\n395: signature must be obtained from a successful pinned Lean probe and compared by\\n396: the auditor; an arbitrary string called `signature` is not verification.\\n397: \\n398: The checker cannot establish that a natural-language formula is well typed,\\n399: that two spellings have the same mathematical meaning, that a hidden theorem\\n400: was not used, that a local witness did not escape the prose, or that an argument\\n401: is valid. Those are explicit Audit A obligations. Neither a JSON PASS nor a\\n402: fabricated complete audit receipt is a mathematical certificate.\\n403: Numbered-reference diagnostics reject common opaque references, not every\\n404: possible paraphrase or hidden import. Behavioral tests must actually ask an\\n405: auditor to review unseen controls; manually prefilled FAIL records only test\\n406: receipt rejection and must be reported as such.\\n407: \\n408: ## Mathematical background receipt extension\\n409: \\n410: New receipts declare `background_policy: \\\"mathematical-background-v2-1\\\"`.\\n411: Each R may contain background_notes with precise mathematics, local grounds and\\n412: actual justification; native lookup is not required. A new supplemented replay\\n413: uses fresh_packet_with_mathematical_background. Optional existing native notes\\n414: retain their complete bound library evidence; mathematical/elementary notes\\n415: carry no native source claim. The authored graph remains unchanged. Other\\n416: variant consumers must reject unsupported receipts instead of dropping fields.\\n417: \\n418: `nl0.validate` returns the note supplement/hash and note count. A packet read\\n419: from an accepted source includes its notes and the original reviewed-packet\\n420: hash. This enriched view is not claimed to be the original replay input.\\n421: Translation binds the supplement in `contract.source.background_supplement_sha256`.\\n422: \\n423: The interface source also retains the complete `background_supplement`, not\\n424: just its hash. Plan steps list the relevant qualified note IDs, for example\\n425: `background_notes:[\\\"R004.BG001\\\"]`. Workflow freeze checks the paragraph mapping;\\n426: local Lean packets receive only their selected notes.\"}"}
{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/nl0-authoring.md\", \"total_lines\": 203, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 203, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 78, \"requested_end\": 250, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # Construct NL0 against Audit A\\n2: \\n3: [Audit A's shared requirements](audit-a.md#shared-mathematical-acceptance-requirements)\\n4: are the sole mathematical acceptance authority. This document is an execution\\n5: guide. Follow the table IDs, not an independently maintained approximation of\\n6: what the auditor might want. The clean carrier supplies that exact frozen\\n7: section to both roles. [The schema](nl0-format.md) describes serialization;\\n8: [the author template](../templates/authoring.md) gives tool shapes and examples.\\n9: The elementary permissions come from the same controller-owned foundation.\\n10: Use ordinary rigorous mathematical granularity. Explicitly register important\\n11: tools; eligible tiny background uses can be checked and recorded by Audit A\\n12: under [mathematical-background admission](background-notes.md). Do not pre-emptively\\n13: rebuild elementary theories or treat an omitted basic M ID as a substantive gap.\\n14: \\n15: ## 1. Understand and freeze the supplied work\\n16: \\n17: Read the original target and entire supplied proof. Identify its phases and\\n18: potential gaps, without adopting an alternative research route. Extract the\\n19: original H, parameters and definition pairs into spec.json. Preserve their\\n20: source locations. This supplies the `target` check; naming a condition inside\\n21: a parameter type does not satisfy its H inventory. Inspect material endpoint,\\n22: zero-object and ambient-dimension cases before building the graph.\\n23: Each H must itself state the complete given condition; phrases such as\\n24: \\\"as specified in the target\\\" do not supply that condition to a packet reader.\\n25: When an original GIVEN passage is already adequate, select its exact frozen\\n26: lines for `spec.hypotheses[i].statement`, using the same literal binding as D\\n27: definitions and S text. Preserve its origin and distinguish original givens\\n28: from desired conclusions and claims established later in the proof. Copying\\n29: does not turn those latter claims into hypotheses or certify fidelity.\\n30: \\n31: Inventory each object's construction guards before exporting it with a type.\\n32: If a guard is a result of this work, establish it in an earlier N producer\\n33: before using the guarded D; do not promise that a later argument will make the\\n34: current declaration well-defined. Alternatively specify an actually total\\n35: construction and prove its intended property separately. Do not add a missing\\n36: guard as an original H. This is graph organization along the given proof,\\n37: not permission to change that proof or its hypotheses.\\n38: When the original already gives a complete adequate definition, use the\\n39: explicit frozen-input line binding for D.definition if helpful. This preserves\\n40: the selected text and its provenance without granting a property. Rewriting\\n41: notation still requires a complete faithful definition and its declarations.\\n42: Use the [complete neutral construction pattern](../templates/guarded-definition-example.md)\\n43: when P includes both a well-definedness claim and a property of the object:\\n44: prove the guard without the object, then use that guard as a direct input to\\n45: the object-using inference. A global list's textual order is not graph order.\\n46: \\n47: ## 2. Design outputs from their consumers\\n48: \\n49: Use the [NL0 constructor](nl0_constructor.md) for N/R partitioning, complete\\n50: consumer-facing exports, workload balance and local S/F organization. Apply it\\n51: while constructing the actual arguments and factual grounds below.\\n52: \\n53: ## 3. Construct the proof and its factual grounds together\\n54: \\n55: An isomorphism or structural identification supplies its stated maps and\\n56: relations, not every subsequent dimension/rank claim by name alone. For a\\n57: needed count, display the basis, spanning/independence and finite count, or\\n58: invoke an appropriate verified source. Likewise regularity closure and\\n59: differentiation rules need their actual grounds; a familiar operation name\\n60: does not extend the fixed calculation profile. Prefer an adequate verified\\n61: background input to unnecessarily rebuilding its whole theory.\\n62: \\n63: For each S, identify its substantive direct inputs and local grounds while\\n64: writing. Ordinary correct elementary reasoning may be compressed; routine mathematical facts may receive concise mathematically reviewed\\n65: background notes, without exact Mathlib lookup. Check the\\n66: unit at that point; do not postpone its source inventory until after the whole\\n67: proof. Read `dependency_closure` and `argument` as construction obligations, not final assurances to fill in later.\\n68: \\n69: If a fact needs an M/E, inspect the real pinned/primary source, register its\\n70: full contract, and write its actual application. Follow `sources` and\\n71: `applicability`: substitute the actual function/domain/scalars and ground each\\n72: premise in an available input or displayed derivation. A source-hypothesis\\n73: field saying a condition holds is not itself a derivation. Prefer an already\\n74: proved adequate general theorem to rebuilding it. A specialized bridge still\\n75: needs its local proof. For E follow [external-source review](external-theorems.md).\\n76: For inaccessible text follow Audit A's [retrieval/deferred checks](audit-a.md#paywalled-or-inaccessible-primary-text).\\n77: \\n78: M is optional. Do not search native declarations for every routine fact before\\n79: NL0 review; use precise mathematical reasoning and the background-note policy.\\n80: If M is explicitly selected, use the actual pinned declaration as the full source contract, including\\n81: its binders, implicit arguments and typeclass premises. Do not insert an\\n82: extra condition into that contract or omit a native premise. Keep a specialized\\n83: application distinct from the native statement and justify its substitutions.\\n84: The probe supplies canonical source metadata; it does not discharge the\\n85: application's premises or certify the local proof.\\n86: \\n87: Write the exact conclusion obtained at an application before using it at a\\n88: different domain, measure, carrier or codomain. Any restriction, extension or\\n89: identification requires its own available ground. If instead deriving a fact\\n90: by a named property's definition, supply that definition and display the\\n91: instantiated clause and ensuing reasoning. Do not register a new source just\\n92: because a fully displayed definition-based derivation also has a standard\\n93: name. Prove each added consequence under all its cases; omit an unnecessary\\n94: extra claim rather than leave its proof incomplete.\\n95: \\n96: Introduce an auxiliary object at its actual first use, with its kind,\\n97: existence basis and scope. Keep object dependencies in `objects` and earlier\\n98: proof IDs in `from_local`. Preserve the object's type and all uses when\\n99: renaming it. Apply `definitions` and the grouped `statement_closure` check to the actual prose.\\n100: Use [logical packaging](logical-rules.md) for quantified and conditional work.\\n101: \\n102: ## 4. Inspect the actual stored candidate\\n103: \\n104: Use check_draft for structural feedback and read_draft for actual changed\\n105: records. Continue from the same-stage candidate after a rejected edit. Stable\\n106: unique @ID/@symbol selectors are available in own-stage reads/edits, not in\\n107: Audit A evidence pointers. Format work requires a concrete edit or source\\n108: query rather than repeated progress reports.\\n109: For multiline text use read_draft's numbered line view and its complete-string\\n110: hash with replace_lines; avoid retyping an escaped old paragraph merely to\\n111: make a small edit. Re-read after a stale hash and use existing @IDs after\\n112: array changes. No editor infers missing mathematics.\\n113: \\n114: Read each own conditional packet after structural checking. It supplies only\\n115: that R's actual premises, definitions, fixed logic, output and own argument.\\n116: Use the shared criteria to inspect these contents, including each output's\\n117: consumer-facing statement, rather than remembering what the full manuscript\\n118: or another producer established. This is the same author's inspection, not an\\n119: extra independent reviewer and not a second Audit A.\\n120: Apply Audit A's shared scope rule: an actual direct N is assumed in this\\n121: conditional implication; do not re-prove its producer or read an ancestor\\n122: body here. The global graph must still contain that producer, and a needed\\n123: fact absent from the exported statement must be established explicitly.\\n124: In the existing original_context_note explicitly check the actual D records'\\n125: types and guards against their available producers, as well as H/target and\\n126: source fidelity. In the inference notes compare each source's exact delivered\\n127: conclusion with the next line that consumes it. These are inspections of the\\n128: stored proof, not another copy of its argument.\\n129: \\n130: Record concise located context/output/inference notes using the existing\\n131: fields. Their criterion grouping comes directly from Audit A. Report readiness\\n132: as no_known_issue, unresolved or not_checked and preserve located open issues.\\n133: A complete set of notes with an unresolved issue cannot release the proof.\\n134: Resolve it in the actual stored argument, or explicitly justify withdrawing\\n135: an erroneous self-finding; reference the resolution location. Do not erase an\\n136: old issue by omitting it. Python validates these states and bindings, not the\\n137: truth of the author's mathematical judgment. A claim of no_known_issue can\\n138: still fail the subsequent independent Audit A.\\n139: \\n140: ### Routine completion and substantive proof gaps\\n141: \\n142: A needed statement in the supplied proof is initially a proof obligation:\\n143: derive it locally, export a proved N, or supply an exactly verified source.\\n144: The fact that the current draft has not done so yet is not a mathematical\\n145: obstruction. Missing inventory, source registration or exposition is work to\\n146: complete, not by itself a terminal gap report. If reporting an actual obstacle,\\n147: identify the concrete implication and the mathematical difficulty encountered\\n148: during normal understanding, checking and completion, rather than only noting\\n149: that no proof/source has yet been entered. This imposes no fixed time/attempt\\n150: count and requires neither a counterexample nor a proof of irreparability.\\n151: \\n152: When writing NL0, expand the argument along the proof strategy already present\\n153: in the original work. Actively supply routine omitted derivations, correct\\n154: verifiable typographical errors and non-substantive local mistakes, and write\\n155: the complete argument into NL0 without interrupting the user for each item.\\n156: Completion must preserve the original target, the original theorem hypotheses\\n157: and the core proof strategy.\\n158: \\n159: If a step remains blocked after normal understanding, checking and completion\\n160: attempts, carefully assess the obstacle: is it work needed for ordinary\\n161: rigorous expansion, or a substantive gap in the original proof? The model\\n162: should make this judgment from the mathematical content and actual progress;\\n163: no fixed elapsed-time limit or attempt count is a mathematical criterion.\\n164: \\n165: If the obstacle is judged to be a substantive proof gap, stop NL0 construction\\n166: for the current proof, preserve the completed work, and report the gap's\\n167: location, the unsupported implication and the grounds for that judgment.\\n168: Do not automatically start new research on the original problem, seek a new\\n169: core proof strategy, or modify the original theorem to make the proof pass.\\n170: \\n171: Recognizing a gap does not require finding a counterexample or proving that\\n172: the gap is irreparable. If the only established fact is that completion has\\n173: not been achieved, and its substantive nature cannot be judged reliably,\\n174: report an \\\"unresolved suspected proof gap\\\" rather than declaring the original\\n175: theorem false. Prolonged runtime, network or tool faults, and model capability\\n176: limitations alone are not evidence of a mathematical error.\\n177: \\n178: In the isolated NL0 carrier, unfinished source registration, JSON bookkeeping or\\n179: ordinary drafting calls for the next actual query, draft or local edit, not an\\n180: obstacle report. Keep the own draft and continue within its remaining budget. A\\n181: terminal mathematical-gap report must identify a genuinely unsupported\\n182: mathematical implication and its grounds, rather than merely restating that\\n183: the requested artifact is not finished. A stage outcome is not whole-task\\n184: completion; ordinary failed candidates return to bounded correction/review.\\n185: \\n186: Incorporate completed corrections into the argument and its actual dependency\\n187: records. Audit A reviews that revised version; existing hash binding and\\n188: invalidation rules still apply.\\n189: \\n190: \\n191: ## 5. Freeze and hand over\\n192: \\n193: When there is no known unfinished obligation, submit the exact candidate bound\\n194: to the author notes and shared acceptance version. If the budget ends first,\\n195: preserve the draft as unfinished; do not call a known-incomplete artifact a\\n196: complete proof. A diagnostic audit of an unfinished draft, if requested,\\n197: remains separate from a qualifying first-acceptance audit.\\n198: \\n199: Freeze source/graph hashes and generate views and an UNVERIFIED scaffold.\\n200: Audit A receives the original problem for fidelity and fresh per-R packets for\\n201: local replay; it receives no author notes or prior review feedback. Its actual\\n202: judgments determine acceptance under the same shared criteria. Structural\\n203: validation, author readiness and mathematical acceptance are distinct results.\"}"}

