{text:"Fix a real mean-zero q in H^gamma and assume L1: ||q||_(H^gamma)<=R. H001-H003 give T>0, 0<gamma<=1 and R>0. Use E001 for source datum U0(x)=-q(-x), exponent s=gamma and horizon T. Reflection and sign preserve the Fourier Sobolev and L2 norms, so ||U0||_(dot H^gamma)<=R and ||U0||_2<=R. The source yields a global unique continuous flow, its uniform Sobolev bound, and smooth flows when the datum is smooth (apply its positive regularity statements for each integer exponent in that case). Its Galerkin bound and limiting-solution bound are uniform for the prescribed norm bounds.",uses:["H001","H002","H003","E001"],objects:["T","gamma","R","q","U0","H","Fourier"],intro:["L1"],facts:[{id:"F001",statement:"For the source datum U0(x)=-q(-x) there is a unique continuous homogeneous H^gamma source flow U on [0,T], with uniform bound determined only by T,gamma,R; for smooth q it has every Sobolev regularity.",objects:["T","gamma","R","q","U0","H"],assumptions:["L1"]}]},
{text:"Take that source flow U and set u(t,x)=-U(t,-x). At (t,-x), u_t=-U_t, u_x=U_x and u_xxx=U_xxx, so u_t+u_xxx=-U U_x=u*u_x. Fourier coefficients are hat u(k)=-hat U(-k), preserving reality, zero mean and every Fourier norm. On mean-zero functions and s>=0, |k|<=<k><=sqrt(2)|k|, hence ||f||_(dot H^s)<=||f||_(H^s)<=2^(s/2)||f||_(dot H^s); for s<0 the inequalities reverse as in the supplied norm convention. The source bound therefore gives some A(T,gamma,R) in our norm. It also holds for a smooth-approximation limit by passing first in each finite Fourier norm sum and then taking the supremum over finite sets. The sign-reflection map is invertible, so uniqueness and continuous dependence transfer. This proves the asserted existence, bound, and solution convention for arbitrary q under L1; discharge L1.",prev:["F001"],objects:["T","gamma","R","q","U0","U","u","A","Fourier","H"],close:["L1"]},
{text:"For arbitrary real mean-zero L2 data q1,q2 and B0>=0, temporarily assume L2: ||q1||_2+||q2||_2<=B0. Apply E001's theta=0 dependence statement at time horizon T to -q1(-x),-q2(-x). Their norms and their difference norm are unchanged. The source twists are unitary, so its dependence bound gives sup_[0,T]||u_q1-u_q2||_2<=C(T,B0)||q1-q2||_2 in physical variables after sign-reflection. Discharge L2. Finally differentiation of v=S_(-t)u cancels the linear KdV term and yields v'=B_t(v,v)/2 for smooth data; the unimodular Fourier symbol of S preserves all norms. Thus M=1+sup||v||_(H^gamma)<=1+A as asserted.",uses:["H001","E001"],objects:["T","B0","q1","q2","H","S","B"],intro:["L2"],close:["L2"]}
