{text:"Fix tau>0 and real alpha,beta. If either phase is zero, substitution in eta shows exact cancellation. Otherwise put h(s)=exp(-i*alpha*s)-M_tau(exp(-i*alpha*s)). The covariance is tau^(-1)integral_0^tau h(s)exp(-i*beta*s)ds. By the fundamental theorem of calculus, |exp(-i*alpha*s)-exp(-i*alpha*r)|<=|alpha||s-r|. Averaging in r gives |h(s)|<=tau|alpha|, and consequently |eta|<=tau|alpha|. Interchange alpha,beta to obtain the minimum. Also |h(0)|+|h(tau)|<=tau|alpha| and integral_0^tau|h'(s)|ds=tau|alpha|. Integrating the beta exponential once by parts gives |eta|<=(|h(0)|+|h(tau)|+integral|h'|)/(tau|beta|)<=2|alpha/beta|. Interchanging phases proves the other ratio. These arguments apply to smooth scalar exponentials, so the calculus identities have no limiting issue.",objects:["tau","alpha","beta","h","eta"]},
{text:"Fix nonzero integer frequencies a,b,c,d with sum zero, order their magnitudes N1>=N2>=N3>=N4, and put A=|ab|, B0=|cd|. Triangle inequality gives N1<=3N2. For the first majorant, if d is among the largest two, min(A/B0,B0/A)<=1 and 1/|d|<=1/N3. Otherwise the two largest are among a,b,c. If they are a,b, use (B0/A)/|d|=|c|/|ab|<=1/N3. If they are a,c, their ratio lies in [1/3,3], giving (1/|d|)min(A/B0,B0/A)<=3 min(|b|/|d|^2,1/|b|)<=3/max(|b|,|d|)=3/N3; the last inequality follows by dividing into |b|<=|d| and its complement. The b,c case is the identical exchange of a,b. For the second majorant, if the largest two lie in a single pair (a,b) or (c,d), the other pair sum has magnitude <=2N3, whereas max(A,B0)>=N1N2>=N3^2. Since a+b=-(c+d), the ratio is <=2/N3. If they lie in different pairs, the third largest is paired with one of them, so max(A,B0)>=N2N3 while |a+b|<=2N1<=6N2. This gives 6/N3. Ties admit any ordering and the cases still exhaust all possibilities.",objects:["a","b","c","d","N1","N2","N3","N4","A","B0"]}
